instruction:
. You are working in the Go repository checked out at /echo (the Echo web framework). Do NOT use the internet. Do NOT look at other installed copies of the code. Only read/edit files under /echo. Do NOT edit or delete any *_test.go file. Do NOT add new features or enhancements; only fix the bug described. IMPORTANT: other agents are concurrently editing /echo/context.go, /echo/binder.go and /echo/middleware/proxy.go — do not touch those files.

BUG REPORT (from a user): "I have an endpoint that returns an empty string with status 200, and I turned on gzip compression for my server; my client sends `Accept-Encoding: gzip`. I expected the response to be a valid gzip stream with `Content-Encoding: gzip` and `Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8`, so that clients decompress it to an empty body. Instead, the response has no `Content-Encoding` header, but the body is still binary gzip data (it starts with the gzip magic bytes), so my HTTP client shows garbage or fails to parse it as text. Here is the handler:
```
e.GET("/empty", func(c echo.Context) error {
    return c.String(http.StatusOK, "")
})
```"

YOUR JOB: Investigate /echo/middleware/compress.go (the gzip response writer / Write path, likely the lazy header/Content-Encoding handling and the empty-body case) plus in-repo documentation/comments stating expected behavior. Find the root cause and fix it minimally and idiomatically so the response is consistent: either a valid gzip stream WITH `Content-Encoding: gzip`, or (if the repo's design says empty bodies are not compressed) no gzip bytes at all. Prefer the behavior that keeps pre-existing tests passing and is most consistent with the repository's own documented contract. Read middleware/compress_test.go to understand expected behavior; do NOT modify tests. If a pre-existing test conflicts with the fix, report it and do NOT break the test.

VERIFY: `cd /echo && go build ./... && go test -count=1 ./...` must pass (all pre-existing tests green). Also write a throwaway program OUTSIDE the repo (e.g. /tmp/compresscheck) using a `replace github.com/labstack/echo/v4 => /echo` go.mod that starts an echo server with `e.Use(middleware.Gzip())`, registers the handler above, performs a real HTTP request with `Accept-Encoding: gzip` and no automatic decompression, and prints the status, the `Content-Encoding` and `Content-Type` headers, and the raw body bytes (hex). Also test a non-empty body for comparison. `go` is at /usr/local/go/bin/go (not on default PATH).

REPORT BACK: the file(s) and exact diff you made, the root-cause explanation, the evidence (program output), and the exact `go test ./...` output.
